  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Using Product rule to find the derivatives</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find the following derivatives of \(\displaystyle f(x) ={@f(x)@}\).</p>
<p>\(\quad \displaystyle f'(x)=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>\(\quad \displaystyle f''(x)=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p> Inorder to find the derivative of \( \displaystyle f(x) = {@f(x)@}\) we will proceed as follows:</p>

  <p>Let \(u(x) =  {@func1@}\) and \(v(x) = {@func2@}\).</p>
  <p>Differentiating \(u\) and \(v\) with respect to \(x\) respectively we get;</p>


<p>
\(u'(x) = {@diff_func1@}\) and \(
v'(x) =  {@diff_func2@}
\)
</p>

 <p>Applying  product rule formula:</p>
<p> \((uv)' = u'v + uv'\)<p>
\(
({@f(x)@})' = ( {@diff_func1@}) \cdot ({@func2@}) + ({@func1@}) \cdot ( {@diff_func2@})
\)
</p>


  <p>Simplifying the expression we get;</p>

<p>
\(
( {@f(x)@})' = {@ta@}
\)
</p>

<p>Therefore the first derivative of \( f(x)={@f(x)@}\) is:</p>
<p>\( f'(x)= {@ta@}\).</p>
<p>For the second derivative \( f''(x)\) we will still apply the product rule formula and repeat the same steps</p>
<p>Therefore the second derivative is \( f''(x)= {@ta2@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
